<article class="env-remark" id="01UU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01UU">Remark <span data-tag="01UU">29.33.6</span>.</a> The lemma above gives a second way of constructing the module of differentials. Namely, let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. Consider the collection of all affine opens $U \subset X$ which map into an affine open of $S$. These form a basis for the topology on $X$. Thus it suffices to define $\Gamma (U, \Omega _{X/S})$ for such $U$. We simply set $\Gamma (U, \Omega _{X/S}) = \Omega _{A/R}$ if $A$, $R$ are as in Lemma <a href="/tag/01UT" data-tag="01UT">29.33.5</a> above. This works, but it takes somewhat more algebraic preliminaries to construct the restriction mappings and to verify the sheaf condition with this ansatz. </p>
</article>